---
# Bootstrap credentials for OpenBao's awskms auto-unseal seal.
#
# This is the one secret in the cluster that cannot come from OpenBao itself.
# Everything else is an ExternalSecret resolved through OpenBao; this one is
# what lets OpenBao unseal in the first place, so it has to exist before there
# is anything to resolve it with.
#
# ArgoCD does not sync this file -- a directory source only picks up .yaml,
# .yml and .json, so the .template extension keeps it out of the Application
# while leaving it next to the thing it configures.
#
# Create it once, by hand, on a freshly provisioned cluster:
#
#   1. Create a symmetric encryption key in AWS KMS and give it the alias the
#      seal stanza in application.yaml refers to:
#
#        aws kms create-key --description "OpenBao auto-unseal (homelab)"
#        aws kms create-alias \
#          --alias-name alias/openbao-unseal \
#          --target-key-id <key-id>
#
#   2. Create an IAM user for OpenBao with only these three actions on that one
#      key -- not on "*". OpenBao needs no other AWS permission:
#
#        kms:Encrypt, kms:Decrypt, kms:DescribeKey
#
#   3. Fill in the access key below and apply it:
#
#        kubectl apply -f kms-credentials.yaml
#
#   4. Delete the local file. The values live in the cluster from then on, and
#      a copy on a laptop is a copy of the thing that decrypts the root key.
#
# The IAM user here is deliberately separate from the one cert-manager uses for
# Route53. They have different blast radii: losing the Route53 key lets someone
# mint certificates for the zone, losing this one lets someone decrypt the
# OpenBao root key given a copy of the Raft data.
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: openbao-kms
  namespace: openbao
type: Opaque
stringData:
  access-key-id: "AKIA..."
  secret-access-key: "..."
